    == Jakarta Data and Hibernate ORM

    Hibernate Data Repositories is an implementation of the Jakarta Data
    specification targeting relational databases and backed by Hibernate ORM.
    Standard Jakarta Data repositories delegate persistence operations to a
    Hibernate `StatelessSession`.

  %img{:src => "/images/repo.png", :alt => 'Repository'}
  %p
  :asciidoc
    In the example above, the Jakarta Data annotations `@Repository`, `@Find`,
    `@Query`, and `@OrderBy` are used together with link:/validator/[Validation]
    annotations like `@NotNull` and `@NotBlank`.

.feature-block
  :asciidoc
    == Minimal magic

    When you compile a repository interface,
    link:/orm/processor[Hibernate Processor] generates an implementation of
    the interface, meticulously checking for mistakes and inconsistencies,
    and reporting them using easily understandable compiler error messages.

    The generated code is simple and extremely readable, and uses only public
    APIs of Hibernate ORM. It's no more difficult to debug a repository than
    it is to debug your own hand-written Hibernate code.

.feature-block
  :asciidoc
    == Compatible with existing entities and queries

    Entity classes are mapped using the familiar annotations defined by Jakarta
    Persistence, and queries may be written in the Hibernate Query Language, a
    superset of the Jakarta Data Query Language (JDQL). If you have existing
    entities and queries that already work in Hibernate ORM, you can use them
    without change in Hibernate Data Repositories.

    On the other hand, the programming model for interacting with the database
    is quite different in Jakarta Data from the model you might be used to from
    Jakarta Persistence.
.feature-block
  :asciidoc
    == Compile-time type safety

    Repository interfaces enable complete compile-time type safety for queries
    and other persistence operations. Hibernate Data Repositories includes a
    complete syntax validator and type checker for HQL, JPQL, and JDQL. Find
    mistakes in your queries immediately, _without_ needing to run any tests.

